WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.491 --> 00:00:01.324
- [Announcer] Ladies and gentlemen,

00:00:01.324 --> 00:00:05.491
give it up for the host
of today's show, Jay Leno.

00:00:06.704 --> 00:00:11.704
(audience cheering)
(upbeat music)

00:00:12.701 --> 00:00:14.100
- Thank you very much!

00:00:14.100 --> 00:00:19.100
(audience cheering)
(upbeat music)

00:00:19.734 --> 00:00:20.997
Thank you.

00:00:20.997 --> 00:00:21.830
That's very kind.

00:00:21.830 --> 00:00:23.497
Thank you very much.

00:00:24.670 --> 00:00:27.337
Thank you, thank you, thank you.

00:00:30.690 --> 00:00:31.590
I can see your disappointment,

00:00:31.590 --> 00:00:34.720
you cover it well, but
thank you, thank you.

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:37.100
Well, let me tell you, Kelly
is taking a personal day.

00:00:37.100 --> 00:00:39.030
She's a mom and we're
getting to the holidays.

00:00:39.030 --> 00:00:40.990
When you have kids, you guys
all know how that works.

00:00:40.990 --> 00:00:43.340
So she asked grandpa Jay to come in,

00:00:43.340 --> 00:00:45.230
and fill in a little bit.

00:00:45.230 --> 00:00:48.520
Now, some of you might know,
Kelly and I have history.

00:00:48.520 --> 00:00:50.178
Not that kind of history.

00:00:50.178 --> 00:00:51.800
(audience laughs)

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.540
Mine was the first talk show she ever did.

00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:54.830
And when she got this show,
she called me and said,

00:00:54.830 --> 00:00:56.290
"What advice do you have?"

00:00:56.290 --> 00:00:58.110
So I kind of passed on some of the advice

00:00:58.110 --> 00:01:01.920
that Johnny Carson gave me, and
it served us both very well.

00:01:01.920 --> 00:01:04.110
One of my favorite things
that Johnny told me was,

00:01:04.110 --> 00:01:06.700
he said, "No good deed goes unpunished."

00:01:06.700 --> 00:01:07.917
You ever heard that expression?

00:01:07.917 --> 00:01:09.099
(audience murmurs in agreement)

00:01:09.099 --> 00:01:11.210
And I never really
understood what that meant.

00:01:11.210 --> 00:01:13.480
Tell me what I did wrong here, okay.

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.313
I'm going to tell you something

00:01:14.313 --> 00:01:16.170
that happened to me a couple months ago.

00:01:16.170 --> 00:01:17.640
I'm helping my wife at the dry cleaner.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.350
I'm carrying all this
stuff in the dry cleaner,

00:01:19.350 --> 00:01:20.460
and I put it on the counter,

00:01:20.460 --> 00:01:22.250
and my wife is one of
those people who likes

00:01:22.250 --> 00:01:24.410
to explain every stain to the dry cleaner.

00:01:24.410 --> 00:01:25.243
Well, this is red,

00:01:25.243 --> 00:01:26.898
when the Harringtons came over this.

00:01:26.898 --> 00:01:29.150
(Jay mumbles)
(audience laughs)

00:01:29.150 --> 00:01:30.770
I said, "Honey, I'm
gonna wait in the car."

00:01:30.770 --> 00:01:31.881
So I'm waiting in the car.

00:01:31.881 --> 00:01:34.240
So I'm sitting in my car, and over here,

00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:37.337
first I hear a woman admonishing a child.

00:01:37.337 --> 00:01:38.527
"I told you to behave!"

00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:41.710
Unruly four or five-year-old, I guess.

00:01:41.710 --> 00:01:43.610
I look over and I see,

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:44.670
the car's kind of filthy,

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.040
like it's been under a tree or something.

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:48.360
And I hear the woman yell at the kid,

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:50.620
and I notice the two rear doors are open.

00:01:50.620 --> 00:01:52.340
And on the ground, on
the passenger's side,

00:01:52.340 --> 00:01:53.650
a big pile of clothes.

00:01:53.650 --> 00:01:55.020
On the ground, on the driver's side,

00:01:55.020 --> 00:01:56.140
a big pile of clothes.

00:01:56.140 --> 00:01:57.390
The trunk is open,

00:01:57.390 --> 00:01:59.080
a big pile of clothes on the ground there,

00:01:59.080 --> 00:02:00.490
with bedding and stuff.

00:02:00.490 --> 00:02:03.130
And one of those big-wheel
bikes sticking out of the trunk.

00:02:03.130 --> 00:02:05.980
And I go, "Boy, this looks kind of bad."

00:02:05.980 --> 00:02:08.400
So I'm watching this right,
I reach in my pocket,

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:09.883
I got a $20 bill.

00:02:11.010 --> 00:02:13.310
So as I go up to the lady
as she's yelling at the kid,

00:02:13.310 --> 00:02:15.270
I go, "Hey, can I give
you a helping hand there?"

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:16.120
She goes, "What?"

00:02:16.970 --> 00:02:19.187
I said, "Can I give you a helping hand?"

00:02:20.510 --> 00:02:21.343
She goes, "Jay,

00:02:21.343 --> 00:02:23.001
"you know my husband owns
the dry cleaner, right?"

00:02:23.001 --> 00:02:25.834
(audience laughs)

00:02:27.118 --> 00:02:28.220
I go, "What are you talking about?"

00:02:28.220 --> 00:02:29.758
She goes, "What'd you
think, we're homeless?"

00:02:29.758 --> 00:02:31.230
(audience laughs)

00:02:31.230 --> 00:02:33.470
I said, "Well, quite honestly,
yeah, I did, I mean"--

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.810
(audience laughs)

00:02:34.810 --> 00:02:36.007
I said "The clothes are on the ground,

00:02:36.007 --> 00:02:37.360
"and the car's kind of dirty."

00:02:37.360 --> 00:02:38.918
And now she's just getting madder.

00:02:38.918 --> 00:02:39.751
(audience laughs)

00:02:39.751 --> 00:02:41.060
So I'm trying to talk.

00:02:41.060 --> 00:02:43.030
And then I see my wife come
out with the dry cleaner.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:44.240
And the dry cleaner goes, "Hi honey!"

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:46.420
She goes, "Maybe if you got
the car washed last week,

00:02:46.420 --> 00:02:48.470
like I told you, people
wouldn't think we're homeless."

00:02:48.470 --> 00:02:50.209
(audience laughs)

00:02:50.209 --> 00:02:51.042
"Well who thinks we're homeless?"

00:02:51.042 --> 00:02:51.875
"Jay Leno!"

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:56.410
Now my wife is mad, the
guy is mad, the woman.

00:02:56.410 --> 00:02:58.443
What did I do wrong, exactly?

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:00.350
But enough about, look,

00:03:00.350 --> 00:03:01.760
we're all getting ready for the holidays.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.740
You're excited about Thanksgiving,
Christmas, the whole--

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.740
(audience cheering)

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:10.520
I have a memorable Thanksgiving story.

00:03:10.520 --> 00:03:12.823
This seems like, we're
pretty close to the holiday.

00:03:13.765 --> 00:03:16.492
(laughs) Did you ever see
your mom just lose it?

00:03:16.492 --> 00:03:18.040
(audience laughs)

00:03:18.040 --> 00:03:20.010
The first time I was nine years old,

00:03:20.010 --> 00:03:21.270
and my mom was in Scotland.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.610
Very strict woman, very stoic.

00:03:23.610 --> 00:03:26.190
No Jamie, you sit still, you be quiet.

00:03:26.190 --> 00:03:27.410
All that kind of thing, you know?

00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:30.480
And my mom married my dad,
who was an Italian guy.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:33.440
And when you're Italian, every meal,

00:03:33.440 --> 00:03:34.850
especially Thanksgiving.

00:03:34.850 --> 00:03:37.560
My mother went through
three ovens in 12 years.

00:03:37.560 --> 00:03:40.580
Literally burned them
out, just cooking meals.

00:03:40.580 --> 00:03:42.960
So she's getting ready for Thanksgiving.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:44.930
Up at five-thirty, basting the turkey,

00:03:44.930 --> 00:03:48.740
and candied yams, and making
the stuffing, and lasagna.

00:03:48.740 --> 00:03:51.540
My mom would make her own
pasta and then cut it.

00:03:51.540 --> 00:03:52.940
'Cause when you're Italian,

00:03:52.940 --> 00:03:55.167
you're taught the
pilgrims brought lasagna.

00:03:55.167 --> 00:03:57.043
(audience laughs)
Turkey was a side dish.

00:03:57.897 --> 00:04:00.310
So anyway, so my mom
was cooking all of this.

00:04:00.310 --> 00:04:01.500
And we would always have dinner

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.133
about one o'clock,
one-thirty in the afternoon.

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:08.020
So the relatives are all inside,
put the leaf in the table,

00:04:08.020 --> 00:04:09.100
make it bigger.

00:04:09.100 --> 00:04:10.700
We're all sitting around,
and then my dad's sitting.

00:04:10.700 --> 00:04:13.133
And my mom comes in carrying
this 21-pound turkey,

00:04:13.133 --> 00:04:15.990
and her big sweat stains under her arms.

00:04:15.990 --> 00:04:18.250
And she puts the turkey
in front of my dad,

00:04:18.250 --> 00:04:20.870
and she gives my dad the electric knife.

00:04:20.870 --> 00:04:22.324
You remember those stupid things?

00:04:22.324 --> 00:04:23.870
(audience murmurs in agreement)

00:04:23.870 --> 00:04:26.010
So my dad takes the electric
knife, and goes click click.

00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:28.117
And he goes, "It's not plugged in!

00:04:28.117 --> 00:04:30.235
"Do I have to do
everything in this house?"

00:04:30.235 --> 00:04:33.453
Well, that was the wrong thing to say.

00:04:34.410 --> 00:04:38.533
I had no idea turkeys could
fly literally across the room.

00:04:39.620 --> 00:04:41.690
I just saw, my mom went (laughs)

00:04:41.690 --> 00:04:44.030
went crazy, and we just ate lasagna

00:04:44.030 --> 00:04:46.062
because the turkey was
over there in the corner.

00:04:46.062 --> 00:04:46.895
(audience laughs)

